WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.430
So far for every episode, I keep thinking
of this moment from Breaking Bad where Aaron

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.230
Paul keeps shouting: (keep getting away with
it clip).

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:12.920
And the person who keeps getting away with
it is Craig Mazin at making such a great adaption

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.639
with so many wonderful changes.

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:20.100
This episode has a perfect balance of retaining
the best moments from the game while also

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.130
making some necessary and well-fitting changes.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.260
I can go through them chronologically, but
I want to address the biggest and most impactful

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.340
ones first which are Joel and Ellie in this
episode.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.000
We all know that their relationship from the
game to the show is much different in that

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.559
Joel is more closed off towards Ellie.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:41.440
This Joel isn’t 100% the same in comparison
to the game and they are making a more real

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.079
and grounded Joel for the TV show.

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:48.329
Instead of a superhero who kills like a hundred
raiders and infected throughout the game.

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.600
Some people may dislike this inherent change,
but I think it’s necessary for the TV medium

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.920
because games have the freedom of not following
logic at all times.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.969
So giving Joel a new characteristic of panic
attacks I think is really well fitting for

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:02.350
this version of Joel.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.670
It makes him more vulnerable and it shows
a weakness that thus makes him more human.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.540
Joel doubts himself and the more he grows
attached to Ellie, the more he doesn’t think

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.030
he’s up to the task of taking care of her.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.210
Like I don’t think this is Joel just suddenly
being overcome with age because the Joel we

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.030
meet in the beginning of the show is much
more confident.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.600
Instead, these panic attacks are being triggered
by him genuinely being afraid of not being

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.290
able to protect Ellie and him kind of seeing
Sarah in her.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.440
This detail about his character is such a
smart point to add that further punctuates

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.150
how much he cares about Ellie.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.510
I think their confrontation this episode lands
extremely well because there has always been

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:43.540
friction between Joel and Ellie and this scene
is the climax of that conflict.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:48.040
Their dynamic up until this point was essentially
Joel trying his best to avoid this type of

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.040
scenario.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.829
He didn’t want to become attached to Ellie
given how her mission was what killed Tess,

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:58.240
but the inevitability of their bond is the
most endearing aspect of The Last of Us.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.110
Not the violence and action throughout the
game as people say there is a gross lack of

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:03.130
in the show.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.190
The characters and their journeys are what
makes this story so amazing.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:11.180
And I may get some hate for it, but I think
Joel and Ellie’s arc is better in the show,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.000
I said it, although it’s not done yet obviously.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.280
But through this road of conflict and slow
build-up of them getting closer, Joel finally

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.280
becoming the warm surrogate father is immensely
satisfying.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.290
It feels much more earned here and you can
see Joel throughout the entire episode grapple

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:27.680
with this conundrum.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.870
Whether it be in this explosive confrontation,
or through more nuanced and detailed moments.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.300
Which on that front, Pedro Pascal gave an
insane performance this episode.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.310
Both for him and Ellie’s scene and Tommy’s.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.500
Seeing him break down and be this vulnerable
was really surprising to me because Joel never

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.440
really gets to this point in the game.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.450
The show is able to explore a much deeper
character dive of Joel and the journey we

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:55.260
go on with him for this episode is captivating
because of how outstanding Pedro is as an

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.260
actor.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:58.650
And Bella Ramsey essentially matches him at
all times.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.260
Casting wise they have been absolutely perfect
and I saw some people doubt Bella Ramsey early

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.180
on and I think she has more than earned the
role of Ellie.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:11.459
For this entire confrontation and overall
set of events, it plays out differently in

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:12.459
the game.

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:16.319
Personally, I didn’t like how Ellie suddenly
runs off in the video game when she overhears

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:18.989
Joel and Tommy discussing having Tommy take
her.

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:23.760
I feel like or at least in the show at this
point, it would be a very juvenile and impractical

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:24.769
thing to do.

00:03:24.769 --> 00:03:28.489
Especially since Ellie running off in the
game leads to Joel and Tommy having to fight

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:30.770
through tonnes of raiders to get to her.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:34.930
If anything I feel like they kind of used
that plot point in the game to introduce more

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:39.300
gameplay and if it was translated directly
over to the show, then it would have felt

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.300
weird.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.430
Bella Ramsey has been very adult-like and
practical and I can’t see her here just

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.629
running away.

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:49.400
Because the whole thing she’s afraid of
is being alone and she’d be putting herself

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.209
in danger and the loved ones that chase after
her in danger too.

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:57.900
So again, I think the change of having Ellie
embracing the cold reality of Joel leaving

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:02.710
her is more heartbreaking and also leads to
a satisfying moment of Ellie immediately choosing

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.519
Joel to take her when he offers her the choice.

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:09.860
This overall payoff is so gratifying because
of the long road it took to get here.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.689
It makes you feel so warm and moved by their
happy scenes together.

00:04:13.689 --> 00:04:18.639
Just them shooting targets alone I can watch
for hours and they are such an amazing duo.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:22.670
They can both hit the dramatic lows of their
relationship while also portraying an immense

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.880
sense of love between each other.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.110
For this little sequence or travel montage
of sorts, I couldn’t escape the infectious

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.389
happiness that they were constantly exuding.

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:35.460
And although I’ve seen people say that the
pacing really picks up here, I think it makes

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:38.800
for a more tragic ending from that fast pace.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.020
Because Joel and Ellie are finally on loving
terms so the fact that that is suddenly ripped

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.020
away from us makes the ending land even harder.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.990
We were just given the most blissful aspect
of the show, so when the creators take it

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:53.490
away from us and it makes the ending even
more depressing.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.960
Craig Mazin could have written a much longer
journey for Joel and Ellie on their way to

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.380
the firefly outpost, but I think this ending
is significantly more impactful.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.270
The only aspect about the ending I would have
changed would be to make the encounter with

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:09.060
the raiders be a bit more suspenseful, but
that is honestly a nitpick.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.380
The scene plays out completely fine and anyone
who is begging for more action needs to relax.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.759
Because we did just get one of the best and
most insane action sequences that also happened

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:21.199
to be original in the last episode.

00:05:21.199 --> 00:05:25.270
So pacing-wise, there weren't any plot points
the show necessarily skips over by speeding

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:26.660
up their journey a bit.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:30.691
Along with me seeing people already complaining
about how the show gets boring whenever it’s

00:05:30.691 --> 00:05:33.580
just Joel and Ellie out in the wilderness
together talking.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.819
Which again is just wild to me because their
duo is the entire point of the show.

00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:42.600
In this episode, the writers also hit all
the best bits for Joel and Ellie’s dialogue

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:46.720
in the game like Joel saying he wanted to
be a singer and earlier on in the episode

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.550
where Ellie was trying to whistle.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:58.240
So it feels like nothing was really left out.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.620
This attention to detail and understanding
of what makes the game great and while also

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.060
making outstanding changes really blows me
away.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.380
Because there’s also so many other smaller
changes that made me love this episode more

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:09.860
than I thought I would.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.380
Like the opening of the episode where Joel
and Ellie are essentially lost so they try

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.440
to get directions from these natives.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.199
Firstly, I think this offers a more realistic
angle in comparison to the game because there

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:24.440
the time jump drops them off right at the
doorstep to the dam where they meet Tommy.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.420
So it feels very convenient to kind of have
wondered into that situation instead of in

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.990
the show where it’s shown that they are
genuinely lost and have been searching for

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.070
a while.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:37.949
Then secondly, the two characters Craig Mazin
introduces here were surprisingly really funny.

00:06:37.949 --> 00:06:41.610
Like I love how chill the wife was given the
situation and the contrast between her and

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:47.210
her husband was great.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:55.430
The fact that this was just a quick one-off
scene is so impressive from a writing standpoint.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.560
These two characters alone could have carried
a much longer scene if they needed to.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.240
Then the next change was the circumstances
of Joel and Ellie meeting Tommy’s group,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.490
and really the entire first 20 minutes is
original but the moment I wanted to address

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:08.900
was the dog scene.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:12.500
I was not expecting to almost have a panic
attack for this scene.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.330
This moment does feel oddly familiar and I
don't know if I'm forgetting it from the game

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:20.180
or if it’s inspired from elsewhere like
a dog sniffing for drugs, but the amount of

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.669
suspense here really hits you like a truck.

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:26.930
Especially since we witness most of this moment
from the perspective of Joel as he’s losing

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:28.259
his shit.

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:32.460
Again tying back to his panic attacks which
is totally justified because I think anyone

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.520
in this situation would have probably turned
into a puddle.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.009
So the twist in seeing that the dog didn’t
smell the fungus and reveals Ellie playing

00:07:39.009 --> 00:07:41.270
with him was a major relief.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.199
Then for the last overall change that really
enhances the show is the fact that we get

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:46.879
to see Tommy’s community.

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:50.759
We don’t actually get to see this area in
the first game and is an overall great setting

00:07:50.759 --> 00:07:53.720
to have all the dramatic moments play out
for the episode.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:57.400
Along with it offering a good contrast in
the way Ellie has essentially grown up and

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.120
has been living her life.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:02.820
Like the shot of her awkwardly sitting in
the crowd of people watching the movie illustrates

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.080
that point really well.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.259
I also want to go back to Joel and Tommy’s
scene since I kind of breezed over it given

00:08:08.259 --> 00:08:12.889
I was talking about Joel and Ellie, but this
scene is so much more emotionally pungent

00:08:12.889 --> 00:08:14.159
in the show.

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:17.960
Since Joel is being overcome with the fear
of not fully being able to take care of Ellie

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.880
and seeing him break down here was so saddening.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.349
And I love how Craig Mazin actually commits
to this scene because before Joel starts recapping

00:08:25.349 --> 00:08:29.120
the events of what happened, they cut to Ellie
walking around the square.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:32.459
Initially, I thought they were cutting away
like in Game of Thrones where Jon Snow is

00:08:32.459 --> 00:08:36.959
spilling the beans to his sisters, but here
we actually have competent writers to fully

00:08:36.959 --> 00:08:40.339
execute and nail the deep drama that was available.

00:08:40.339 --> 00:08:41.490
So thank you.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:45.480
I said it earlier but the acting from Pedro
is purely astounding.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.519
He honestly moved me to tears in this scene.

00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:52.600
And the fact that we go from this to the Joel
and Ellie confrontation is a pure wombo combo

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:54.660
and probably floored everyone.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:58.640
The next year of the Emmys is going to be
an insane battle between House of the Dragon

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.100
and The Last of Us alone.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:05.470
Overall this episode was another home run
for me and it manages to hit a major emotional

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.260
climax while having a great line-up of scenes.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:14.050
It nails the suspense, the drama, heartbreak,
has a bit of action, heartwarming moments,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:17.970
and a moving ending that acts as a gut punch
since we just saw the light at the end of

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:18.970
the tunnel.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:23.170
The next episode seems to be predominantly
flashback driven with Ellie, which I think

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:27.410
is based on the DLC for the first game, so
I wonder how people will treat that episode.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:31.950
I don’t know if we're going to have another
episode 3 on our hands in terms of audience

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:32.950
reaction.

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.890
Regardless though, thank you for watching,
and let me know what you thought about the

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.170
latest episode of The Last of Us.

